GAN Augmentation for Class-Imbalanced Fraud Detection
Adebola Veralyne Fijabi (1298724) · Tolu Omoniyi (1340808)
COMP 5112 Research Methodology — Lakehead University, Fall 2026
A controlled comparison of augmentation strategies for class-imbalanced
classification. A Generator and Discriminator are implemented from scratch in
PyTorch (WGAN-GP with gradient penalty and gumbel-softmax for categorical
columns), and compared against SMOTE, its variants, and the published CTGAN
reference.
---
The research question
Does synthetic minority data from a GAN improve fraud detection more than
SMOTE, and is any improvement larger than the run-to-run noise?
The experimental design
One variable changes. Everything else is held fixed.
condition	what is added to the training split	classifier
`none`	nothing	random forest
`smote`	SMOTE / SMOTENC interpolated rows	random forest
`borderline_smote`	borderline-region interpolations	random forest
`adasyn`	density-adaptive interpolations	random forest
`tabular_gan`	rows sampled from our WGAN-GP	random forest
`ctgan`	rows sampled from published CTGAN	random forest
The GAN is not a classifier and is never compared against one. It
manufactures training rows. The random forest is the measuring instrument,
held constant so that any difference in the score is attributable to the
augmentation and nothing else.
The same stratified 70/15/15 split, the same selected features, the same
preprocessing fitted on train only, and the same threshold rule apply to every
condition. Augmentation happens after the split and touches the training
split only, so no synthetic row can ever reach validation or test.
