What the application does
Tab	What happens
1 · Data	Load, inspect, set the minority budget, split 70/15/15, select features
2 · Train the GAN	Train Generator and Discriminator, watch the loss curves
3 · Generated data	Sample synthetic rows, run mode-collapse diagnostics, compare distributions
4 · Comparison	Run the conditions, see the results table and charts, export CSV
The minority budget slider
The most important control on the Data tab. It caps how many real minority rows
the generator is allowed to learn from.
This matters because augmentation only helps when real examples are scarce.
On full PaySim, with 5,749 fraud rows in the training split, a random forest
reaches PR-AUC 0.998 on the untreated baseline and no augmentation method can
improve on it. Every condition returns the same number. Lowering the budget to a
few hundred rows creates the regime where the comparison is informative: at a
budget of 150, the baseline falls to 0.879 and the conditions separate.
Sweeping the budget (50, 100, 250, 500, all) produces a curve showing where
generative augmentation begins to pay off, which is a stronger result than a
single comparison.
---
Results obtained
PaySim — 6,362,620 rows, 8,213 fraud (0.129%)
The untreated baseline reaches PR-AUC 0.9981 with recall 0.997 and precision
1.000. No condition improves on it; all return approximately the same value.
This is a real finding, not a failure. PaySim's derived balance-error features
separate the classes almost perfectly for a tree ensemble, so the experiment has
no headroom. Logistic regression on the same data sits near 0.62, which locates
the signal in feature interactions that trees capture and a linear boundary
cannot.
IEEE-CIS — 590,540 rows, 432 features, 20,663 fraud (3.499%), 2 seeds
condition	PR-AUC	MCC	recall	precision	seconds
none	0.6074 ± 0.0091	0.5741 ± 0.0208	0.527	0.656	40
smote	0.6043 ± 0.0106	0.5792 ± 0.0103	0.525	0.669	68
tabular_gan	0.6015 ± 0.0123	0.5718 ± 0.0162	0.518	0.662	799
Neither augmentation method beats the untreated baseline. The baseline's own
seed-to-seed swing is 0.0128, which is larger than SMOTE's deficit of 0.0031 and
the GAN's of 0.0059. Both differences sit inside the noise. The ordering
(none > smote > gan) held across both seeds, and the GAN cost roughly twenty
times the compute of the baseline for no measurable return.
Interpretation
On two datasets with thousands of real minority examples, generative
augmentation did not improve detection. The negative result is the contribution.
The minority-budget sweep shows where the picture changes: below roughly two
hundred real minority rows, augmentation begins to matter, and SMOTE and the GAN
separate from the baseline.
