The model
`gan.py` contains the network definitions.
Generator — noise vector in, candidate row out. Numeric columns pass through
`tanh`; each categorical column gets its own `gumbel_softmax` block. That second
part is what makes GANs work on tables. Selecting a category is a discrete
choice, gradients cannot flow through a discrete choice, so the generator could
never learn. Gumbel-softmax supplies a differentiable almost-one-hot vector
during training and a hard choice at sampling time.
Discriminator — row in, single unbounded score out. No sigmoid: this is a
Wasserstein critic rather than a classifier, which makes training far more
stable on the small minority pools this problem produces. The loss values are
therefore not interpretable as accuracy; only the shape of the curves matters.
Gradient penalty — keeps the critic's gradients near unit norm. This is the
single change that most improves stability and is why the model converges on a
few hundred rows.
Generator diagnostics
Before any synthetic row is used, four checks run:
duplication rate — share of synthetic rows that are near-copies of a real
row. High values mean memorisation rather than learning.
diversity ratio — synthetic spread divided by real spread. Values well
below 1 indicate mode collapse.
coverage — share of real rows with a synthetic neighbour.
marginal similarity — agreement of per-column distributions.
A generator that fails these is not used, regardless of what the downstream
classifier scores.
Evaluation
Accuracy is deliberately not reported. On PaySim, 99.87% of transactions are
legitimate, so a model predicting "legitimate" for everything scores 99.87% and
catches no fraud at all.
Reported instead: precision, recall and F1 for comparability with published
work; PR-AUC as the headline discrimination measure; MCC as the headline
balanced measure; and wall-clock time, so the cost of any gain is visible.
The decision threshold is chosen on the validation split and then frozen
before test is touched.
The one rule
Only `augment.py` differs between conditions. Data collection, splitting,
feature selection, preprocessing and evaluation are shared.
Known limitations
The random split ignores transaction time; `split_by_time` implements the
chronological alternative as a robustness check.
The majority class is capped in the training split for tractability. This is
stated rather than silent; validation and test are never capped.
On datasets with categorical columns, BorderlineSMOTE and ADASYN fall back to
SMOTENC, because imbalanced-learn provides no categorical-aware form of
either. Both then return results identical to plain SMOTE.
With a single seed the reported spread is zero, so the `beats_noise` column is
meaningless. Run at least three seeds before quoting any comparison.
IEEE-CIS results use two seeds, which is below our own stated threshold. The
ordering was stable across both, but the comparison should be read as
indicative rather than settled.
Reproducing
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python -c "
from fraudgan import make_demo, Config, run, table
r = run(make_demo(n=20000, fraud_rate=0.01),
        Config(seeds=[0,1,2], conditions=['none','smote','tabular_gan'],
               classifiers=['random_forest']))
print(table(r))
"
```
Every seed is fixed. Given the same seeds the numbers reproduce exactly.
